# SPI Resolution 2026-09-14.mk.1: Transparency.dev as an associated project **DRAFT**

WHEREAS

1. Transparency.dev is a substantial and significant free and open-source
   software project maintaining public witness network infrastructure for
   tamper-evident logs and related software suite for building end-to-end
   transparency ecosystems.

2. Transparency.dev needs SPI support and assistance with:

   - Transfer, registration, holding and protection of intangible assets
     including trademarks and domain names.
   - Administrative ownership of code repository, social media and community
     service accounts.
   - Project operations vendor contract management including contracting with
     external suppliers for community coordination and managing contracts for
     Transparency.dev Summit.
   - Financial administration including accepting donations, holding funds,
     processing outgoing payments and reimbursements, and financial reporting.

THE SPI BOARD RESOLVES THAT

3. Transparency.dev is formally invited to become an SPI Associated Project
   in accordance with the SPI Framework for Associated Projects, SPI Resolution
   1998-11-16.iwj.1-amended-2004-08-10.iwj.1, a copy of which can be found at
   https://www.spi-inc.org/corporate/resolutions/2004/2004-08-10.iwj.1/

4. Patrick Flynn is recognised by SPI as the authoritative decision maker and
   SPI Liaison for Transparency.dev, with Bob Callaway serving as a deputy
   contact in cases when the Liaison is not available. Successor Liaison and
   deputy contact will be appointed by Transparency.dev Technical Steering
   Committee per documented voting and resolution pathways outlined in the
   project charter.

5. If the last known SPI Liaison for Transparency.dev is unreachable and no new
   SPI Liaison is appointed for two consecutive years the associated project is
   deemed defunct.

6. In such a circumstance the SPI Board will in its good-faith discretion
   proceed as follows: the Board will identify one or more other SPI
   associated projects or 501(c)(3) charities which can accept the project's
   assets and use them for a similar purpose, and transfer the assets to the
   identified destination or destinations as feasible. Assets for which no such
   transfer can be arranged within a reasonable time will become part of the
   SPI general fund.

7. This invitation will lapse, if not accepted, 60 days after it is approved
   by the SPI Board.
